  "Question": "A scout troop buys \\(1000\\) candy bars at a price of five for \\(\\$2.\\) They sell all the candy bars at a price of two for \\(\\$1.\\) What was their profit, in dollars?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(100\\)",
    "B": "\\(200\\)",
    "C": "\\(300\\)",
    "D": "\\(400\\)",
    "E": "\\(500\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Profit is the money received from sales minus the purchase cost. Buying \\(1000\\) bars in groups of five gives \\(1000/5=200\\) groups, each costing \\(2\\) dollars. Selling them in pairs gives \\(1000/2=500\\) pairs, each bringing in \\(1\\) dollar. Thus, in dollars,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Cost}&=200\\cdot2=400,\\\\\n\\text{Revenue}&=500\\cdot1=500.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe profit is \\(500-400=\\boxed{100}\\) dollars, choice \\(\\text{A}\\).",
